From: ylh440104 <ylh440104@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] linux-user: drop translations when the guest says its code changed

cacheflush tells the translator that the bytes in the given range were
rewritten. qemu answers it with a bare success and drops the request, on
the grounds that Dalvik's JIT calls it for every method it compiles and
the invalidation is not cheap.

That reasoning is sound for the JIT and wrong for everyone else. A caller
that assembles code and then enters it through a function pointer is
exactly the caller this syscall exists for, and for it the dropped answer
is a wrong answer. PixelFlinger is such a caller: it builds a scanline
routine in the heap, announces it here, and jumps in.

What that looks like from the outside:

    PC=0x001a84b8  [guest: rw-] in 0000a000-001ad000 rw-p
    qemu: bytes at PC: 43f0 e92d 2344 e590 1348 e590
    qemu: LR=0xacf0afe4  libpixelflinger.so

The address differs on every run, the bytes there are identical, and the
instruction that faults is not one the assembler emits. That is the
signature of entering memory whose translation was built before the code
was written into it: the guest executes the old translation, not the
bytes it just produced.

Invalidating unconditionally is cheap where it matters and expensive
where it was already avoided. A freshly written page has no translation
yet, so tb_invalidate_phys_range finds no PageDesc for it and returns
without taking the page collection lock. A page the JIT has already run
has one, and pays. The cost therefore lands on the JIT, which is where
the original decision wanted it, and not on the assembler, which cannot
function without it.

GUEST_TBFLUSH still overrides this for measurement.

Signed-off-by: ylh440104 <ylh440104@gmail.com>

------ a/linux-user/arm/cpu_loop.c	2026-10-27 01:30:59.000000000 +0000
+++ b/linux-user/arm/cpu_loop.c
@@ -481,13 +481,28 @@
                                            env->regs[14], env->regs[15]);
                         }
                         /*
-                         * ★★★ GUEST_NO_SMC: ловушки на запись нет, значит
-                         * переводы сбрасываем ровно по просьбе гостя — он
-                         * называет промежуток, который поменял.
+                         * cacheflush means "the code in this range changed", and the only correct
+                         * answer is to drop the translations built from those bytes. It used to be
+                         * answered with a bare success, on the grounds that Dalvik's JIT calls it for
+                         * every method it compiles and the cost was not worth paying there. But a
+                         * caller that writes code and then jumps into it is exactly the caller this
+                         * syscall exists for, and for it the dropped answer is a wrong answer:
+                         *
+                         *   PixelFlinger assembles a scanline routine into the heap, announces it
+                         *   here, and enters it through a function pointer. With the announcement
+                         *   ignored, the entry runs whatever was translated from that memory before.
+                         *   The crash lands on a heap address, the bytes at that address are the
+                         *   routine the assembler meant, and the instruction that faults is one it
+                         *   never emitted — which is precisely what the reports showed.
+                         *
+                         * The range invalidation is cheap for that caller and not for the JIT. A
+                         * freshly written page has no translation yet, so tb_invalidate_phys_range
+                         * finds no PageDesc for it and returns without touching the page collection;
+                         * a page the JIT has already executed has one, and pays for the lock. Doing
+                         * it unconditionally therefore costs the JIT, and only the JIT.
                          */
-                        if (guest_no_smc()) {
+                        {
                             abi_ulong s = env->regs[0], e = env->regs[1];
-
                             if (e > s) {
                                 mmap_lock();
                                 tb_invalidate_phys_range(s, e - 1);
